**Finding: version 1 under-tiers in exactly the way Phase 1 predicted.** With only the brief and one prompt, the model agreed with the gold tier on 48% of 75 cases (Cohen's kappa 0.22). It under-tiered **15 of 36** high-risk or prohibited cases, including **5 of 9** deliberately understated (adversarial) briefs. Examples:

* the nano-loan app that approves and declines automatically (H2-S) was rated minimal;
* every prohibited case was rated only high, e.g. emotion recognition of call-centre staff (P1) and social scoring (P2);
* most customer-facing chatbots were rated minimal, missing the Art. 50 transparency duty.

A brief alone does not contain enough to tier on. A model reading it takes the team's framing at face value. That is why the final design asks follow-up questions, sets hard minimum tiers in code, and requires a verbatim quote for every fact. (Two baseline calls failed with a Groq "failed to generate JSON" error on gpt-oss-20b, so n = 75 of 77.)
